# The servo-side motion profile, loaded once at process setup and read by both
# control cogs.
#
# Protobuf text of `brenn_reachy.cogs.config_clk_proto.ServoProfile`, which the
# compiler generates from the `ServoProfile` schema in `config.clk`. Read as it
# is: the box binds it by path, and the casing converts it to the message the
# dial hands the cog.
#
# One file, two readers. The session writes these pairs into the nine servos
# when it commissions the machine; the decision tick builds its plant models
# from the same pairs, so what it judges a joint against is the trajectory the
# machine's own generators are running. A second statement of any of these
# numbers is a screen sized for a machine nobody commissioned.
#
# One pair per servo class, stated as six flat keys. The classes are separate
# because their motors and their Velocity Limits are: the antennas' Velocity
# Limit register reads 1620 and the seven head servos' reads 445, so what the
# antennas can be commissioned with is not what the head can. Flat keys rather
# than a nested pair message because every reader here is a first-match
# `key: value` line scan.

# The servo-side motion profile the commission sequencer writes, in the
# registers' own units (0.229 rev/min for velocity, 214.577 rev/min² for
# acceleration).
#
# Two pairs across the three classes, and each one a measurement.
#
# The legs run their own capability: a tour of the whole clip library at
# `(32767, 445)` -- the generator wide open, so the motor and not the generator
# was the binding constraint -- read the class motor-bound at 326 velocity units
# with a goal-step ramp median of 287, and a confirmation tour of the same
# library at 287 / 326 held the six cranks 0.3319 rad from their own modelled
# trajectory at the worst, against a 0.4 rad bound, reading the class
# motor-bound at 326 again.
#
# The body yaw and the antennas run the measured velocity cap of the recorded
# library tour: every joint class chasing a goal more than 0.1 rad away travels
# a median of 0.0230-0.0245 rad per 20 ms period, against the 0.023981 rad the
# velocity 50 converts to, and a joint setting off from rest ramps at the
# acceleration. At that pair those two classes run velocity-saturated -- the
# content asks for 3-20 rad/s against a 1.2 rad/s ceiling -- so the pair is what
# shapes their motion rather than a backstop under it. The body yaw is there
# because its own capability reading is that pair to within the capability
# instrument's ratio: what holds the class back is its loop, not its motor. The
# antennas are there because their measured capability is faster than the plant
# model can be right at -- at the shipped gains the joint reaches its
# generator's speed but follows it about one and a half periods of travel
# behind, and the tracking screen has no room for that lag -- so the fast pair
# stays on record and uncommissioned. The mark is on the schema's own field in
# `cogs/config.clk`.
#
# The bench's trial-validated figures (400/600, including an 855°/s antenna
# sweep and the 0.8 s presence gesture) were sized for a host commanding whole
# moves, where the profile is only the backstop under host-side shaping and a
# tight one throttles the shaped motion itself. The session streams
# step-bounded setpoints instead, one per cycle, which is what the low pair is
# for.
#
# Zero in either register is a servo with no generator at all. Both readers
# refuse it, per class, before anything is commanded.
legs_profile_acceleration: 287
legs_profile_velocity: 326
body_yaw_profile_acceleration: 20
body_yaw_profile_velocity: 50
antennas_profile_acceleration: 20
antennas_profile_velocity: 50
